# `type` vs `interface`

House style (not the TS handbook default):

| Use | For |
|-----|-----|
| `type` | Object **shapes**: DTO, JSON, HTTP/Passport payload, use-case params, test fixtures (`SignUpParams`, `SeededTestUser`, `GoogleOAuthTokenPayload`, `TaskDTO`) |
| `interface` | **Contracts** a class `implements` (`IMailAdapter`, `IAbstractMailFactory`, `UseCase`) |
| `class` | Runtime domain objects (`ValueObject`, entities). Never a `type`/`interface` in `domain/values/` |

`type Foo` is erased at compile time. It does **not** mean class instances of `Foo`. It only describes the shape of a plain object.

```ts
// BAD — payload / DTO as interface
export interface SeededTestUser {
  userId: string;
}

// GOOD
export type SeededTestUser = {
  userId: string;
};

// GOOD — class contract
export interface IMailAdapter {
  sendEmail(...): Promise<void>;
}
```

Do not convert `type` → `interface` "for consistency". Do not convert `interface` → `type` when something is `implements`'d.

`@typescript-eslint/consistent-type-definitions` is **off** in backend ESLint. Its stylistic default prefers `interface` and would `--fix` payloads into interfaces.
